{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/clackamasfire/posts/hat-tip-to-our-partners-at-the-oregon-city-police-department-and-the-clackamas-c/1245812607575861/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/clackamasfire/posts/hat-tip-to-our-partners-at-the-oregon-city-police-department-and-the-clackamas-c/1245812607575861/","title":"Clackamas Fire","description":"Hat tip to our partners at the Oregon City Police Department and the Clackamas County District Attorney’s Office for their work investigating this case and securing a conviction.\n\n“We are grateful...","language":"en","text":"## Clackamas Fire's Post\n\n---\n\n### **Clackamas Fire**\n\nDecember 8, 2025\n\nHat tip to our partners at the Oregon City Police Department and the Clackamas County District Attorney’s Office for their work investigating this case and securing a conviction.\n\n“We are grateful for the diligent investigative work by the Oregon City Police Department and the Clackamas County District Attorney’s Office. Their commitment to this case brought accountability for a dangerous act that put firefighters and the community at risk.” - Clackamas Fire Chief Nick Browne\n\nSpecial thanks to the officers, detectives, and prosecutors who helped bring this case to a close, and to the nearly 200 fire personnel who battled this fire and worked tirelessly to protect the community.\n\n#### **Oregon City Police**\n\nDecember 4, 2025\n\nThe man who set fire at Blue Heron paper mill, convicted of arson, was sentenced to 60 months in prison.\n\nToday, Christian Dukes of Oregon City entered a Guilty Plea to intentionally starting the fire at the mill on January 30th, 2025. He was sentenced to 60 months in state prison for the crime.\n\nPlease see some additional information from the Clackamas County District Attorney's Office:\n\nDukes admitted to intentionally setting a fire on Jan. 30, 2025, that destroyed a three-story building at the long-vacant paper mill at the south end of downtown Oregon City. Dukes stacked a pile of paper several feet high on the floor of the wooden building, then ignited it with a Bic lighter.\n\nHe disclosed that he was upset because he believed some of his belongings had been stolen by transients who camped on the property at 419 Main Street.\n\nThe fire suppression effort involved approximately 190 fire personnel. Three firefighters were injured while battling the blaze, which took hours to get under control.\n\n“This fire created a very dangerous situation and put many people in harm's way,” said Deputy District Attorney John Millar, who prosecuted the case.\n\nAdditionally, now that Mr. Dukes has been convicted of the crimes he was arrested for, his mugshot is now releasable under Oregon Law (House Bill 3273 - 2022)\n\nSpecial thanks to OCPD Officers Hanes and LaPointe, Detective Wadsworth, Clackamas Fire, and the Clackamas County District Attorney's Office for their hard work and diligence on this investigation.\n\nAll reactions:\n\n8 comments\n\n2 shares\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nMost relevant\n\n\n\nDonald Phillips\n\nHave him help clean up his mess also!\n\n* 47w\n\n4\n\nMundy Clark Alcala\n\nThank you for your work and effort in getting the person responsible\n\n* 47w\n\nSheila Beyette\n\nDoes he need to pay also for the value of the mill?\n\nThe kid that set fire to the gorge on accident with fireworks gets to pay reparations, why does a grown man deliberately starting fires not owe money?\n\n* 47w\n\n4\n\nTravis Schweitzer\n\nSheila Beyette the mill was scheduled to be demolished. I think he saved them a lot of money.\n\n* 47w\n\n1\n\nSheila Beyette\n\nTravis Schweitzer point not received. If someone burned down my house I still need a house. He did not own the house. He does not get to get all the fire departments out fighting it. He needs to do time and psy something from his future job to repay the costs of his angry action.\n\n* 47w\n\n1\n\nTravis Schweitzer\n\nSheila Beyette I agree it wasn't right to set fire to the mill. I also agree he needs to do his time in jail. I'm simple pointing out, you argue \"he should pay for the value of the mill\". There was no value. The tribe would be paying him as the cost to demolish it would have been much greater if it had not burnt.\n\n* 47w\n\n2\n\nLinda Myers\n\nSheila Beyette If your house was scheduled for demolition, no one who burned it down would need to pay for it. He's in prison fir 5 years, he is paying.\n\n* 47w\n\nMost relevant is selected, so some comments may have been filtered out.","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":2309.2381700000146,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}